# TIMES(2)

## NAME

times - get process times

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <sys/times.h>

int times(struct tms *buffer);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Times* returns time-accounting information for the current process and for the terminated
child processes of the current process. All times are in 1/HZ seconds. **Note: HZ is 250
here,** which is what this kernel programs the clock to and what CLOCKS_PER_SEC in <time.h> is
defined as.

After the call, the buffer will appear as follows:

```c
struct tms {
        time_t  tms_utime;      /* user time */
        time_t  tms_stime;      /* system time */
        time_t  tms_cutime;     /* user time, children */
        time_t  tms_cstime;     /* system time, children */
};
```

**Note:** v7's page called the struct *tbuffer* and its members *proc_user_time* and so on. The
names here are the ones <sys/times.h> declares, which are the names v7's own kernel and
successors use. The children times are the sum of the children's process times and their
children's times.

## SEE ALSO

time(1), time(2), ctime(3)

**Note:** <sys/times.h> declares *times* now; v7 declared it in no header and every caller
wrote its own extern. The declaration is guarded, because the kernel has a handler of the same
name with a different shape and the two must never both be in scope.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_times** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
